{"slug": "smd-resistor-code", "targets": ["smd resistor value chart"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">SMD Resistor Value Chart for Common 3 Digit and 4 Digit Codes</h2><p>An <strong>smd resistor value chart</strong> lets you match a printed marking to its resistance at a glance. The table below lists popular values with both the 3 digit code, used on 5% parts, and the 4 digit code, used on 1% parts. In both systems the last digit is the multiplier and R marks the decimal point.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Resistance</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">3 digit code (5%)</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">4 digit code (1%)</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1 Ω</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1R0</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1R00</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4.7 Ω</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4R7</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4R70</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10 Ω</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10R0</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100 Ω</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">101</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1000</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">220 Ω</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">221</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">2200</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">330 Ω</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">331</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">3300</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">470 Ω</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">471</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4700</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">102</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1001</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">2.2 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">222</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">2201</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4.7 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">472</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4701</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">103</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1002</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">47 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">473</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4702</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">104</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1003</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1 MΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">105</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1004</td></tr></table></div><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">How to Use the Chart</h3><ol><li>Read the marking with a magnifier, keeping the text upright.</li><li>If it has two letters or a number plus a letter, use the EIA 96 chart instead.</li><li>Otherwise find the code in the matching column above.</li><li>For a value not listed, multiply the first digits by 10 raised to the last digit.</li><li>Confirm with a multimeter if the part is still in circuit, as parallel paths can change the reading.</li></ol><p>Note that 100 is 10 Ω and not 100 Ω, a very common mistake.</p>"}